  "full_response": "DNS resolution is a critical aspect of how the web works. Here's a step-by-step explanation of the process that takes place when you type a URL into your browser:\n\n\n1. When you enter the URL in the browser, the first step is to check if your device is connected to your local Internet connection, such as Wi-Fi or Ethernet, to communicate with the DNS.\n\n\n2. The browser has a built-in resolver, or it contacts your operating system's network stack, to perform the DNS query. This sends a request to a configured DNS server on your local network3. If the request doesn't find the DNS information within the local cache (also known as \"DNS cache\"), it queries your DNS server, which is typically assigned by your Internet Service Provider (ISP) or managed by your organization.\n\n\n4. The router you are connected to then receives this request and forwards it to the Root Name Servers (NS) if the DNS is not found locally. These servers are authoritative for the domains and provide responses with Information about the Top-Level Domain (TLD) Servers, such as .com, .net, .org, etc.\n\n\n5. Your request is then passed to appropriate TLD servers. These servers, in turn, direct the request to the authoritative Name Servers for the exact domain in question.\n\n\n6. The authoritative Name Servers then look up the domain's DNS mapping for the specific URL you are trying to access and respond with the IP address.\n\n\n7. Your computer now has the IP address and can establish a TCP/IP connection with the server where the content you requested is stored.\n\n\n8. The web server processes your request and sends the requested webpage back to your computer over the Internet.\n\n\n9. Finally, your web browser receives the HTML content and renders it for you to view.\n\n\nA technical look at this process introduces concepts like DNS cache poisoning, DNS prefetching, and DNS lookups at various protocol levels. However, this overview captures the high-level steps that occur during DNS resolution.",
